.TH "COMPLYCTL\-PROVIDER\-AMPEL" "1" "November 2026" "ComplyTime Manual"
.SH NAME
complyctl\-provider\-ampel \- Ampel scanning provider for complyctl
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-ampel\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]complyctl\-provider\-ampel\f[R] is a gRPC plugin for
\f[B]complyctl\f[R](1) that provides compliance scanning capabilities
using the Ampel evaluation engine.
It is not invoked directly by users; \f[B]complyctl\f[R] discovers and
launches it automatically when the provider is installed.
.PP
The provider receives assessment configurations from \f[B]complyctl\f[R]
and evaluates repository configurations against granular security
policies (e.g., branch protection rules) using the \f[B]snappy\f[R] and
\f[B]ampel\f[R] CLI tools.
Results include per\-tenet messages and remediation guidance.
.PP
Communication with \f[B]complyctl\f[R] uses the hashicorp/go\-plugin
gRPC interface, implementing three RPCs: \f[B]Describe\f[R],
\f[B]Generate\f[R], and \f[B]Scan\f[R].
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is provided through variables defined in assessment plan
targets.
These variables are passed to the provider via \f[B]complyctl\f[R] and
are not set manually by the user.
.TP
\f[B]url\f[R] (required)
HTTPS URL of the repository to scan.
Must use the HTTPS scheme.
Example: \f[B]https://github.com/org/repo\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]specs\f[R] (required)
Comma\-separated collection spec references for snappy, not policy
identities.
Example: \f[B]builtin:github/branch\-rules.yaml\f[R].
Relative spec file paths resolve against the selected workspace.
Built\-in references pass through to snappy.
Path traversal sequences (\f[B]../\f[R]) are rejected.
.TP
\f[B]branches\f[R] (optional)
Comma\-separated list of branches to scan.
Must match the pattern \f[B][a\-zA\-Z0\-9._/\-]+\f[R].
Path traversal sequences are rejected.
Default: \f[B]main\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]access_token\f[R] (optional)
Authentication token for accessing private repositories.
Rejected if it contains newlines or null bytes.
.TP
\f[B]platform\f[R] (optional)
Platform hint for self\-hosted instances (e.g., \f[B]github\f[R],
\f[B]gitlab\f[R]).
Used when hostname\-based detection fails to identify the platform.
Default: auto\-detected from the URL hostname.
.TP
\f[B]ampel_policy_dir\f[R] (optional, global variable)
Custom directory containing granular Ampel policy source files.
Takes second priority after complypack content.
Default: \f[B].complytime/ampel/granular\-policies/\f[R]
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]COMPLYTIME_MACHINE_ID_FILE\f[R]
Path to the machine\-id file used for host identification in evidence
remarks.
The file content is included in each Evidence.Source.Remarks field
alongside the hostname so auditors can identify the host where the scan
was executed.
Default: \f[B]/etc/machine\-id\f[R]
.SH EXTERNAL TOOLS
Requires \f[B]snappy\f[R] and \f[B]ampel\f[R] CLI tools at runtime.
\f[B]ampel\f[R] version 1.4.2 or later is required for SKIP handling and
the \f[B]\(enallow\-skip\f[R] flag.
The provider verifies the ampel version at
\f[B]Generate\f[R]/\f[B]Scan\f[R] time and reports an actionable error
if the installed version is below the minimum.
.PP
These tools are not currently packaged in Fedora and must be installed
separately.
See the Carabiner project for installation instructions: \c
.UR https://github.com/carabiner-dev
.UE \c
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/\f[R]
Provider artifact directory.
All paths below are relative to the selected workspace root, not the
invoking directory or the Evaluation Log\(cqs directory.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/results/<repository>/<branch>/<spec>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/snappy.intoto.json\f[R]
In\-toto attestation produced by \f[B]snappy snap\f[R].
Contains raw API data collected from the repository (e.g., branch
protection rules).
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/results/<repository>/<branch>/<spec>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/ampel.intoto.json\f[R]
In\-toto attestation produced by \f[B]ampel verify\f[R].
Contains the policy evaluation results against the snappy\-collected
data.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/results/<repository>/<branch>/<spec>/evaluation\-<time>\-<token>/metadata.json\f[R]
Best\-effort supporting context: source identity, original branch and
collection spec, available policy/configuration provenance, evaluation
token and collection start.
Metadata omits credentials and evidence digests; it is not evidence
authority.
.PP
Repository, branch and spec labels are non\-empty ASCII components of at
most 80 bytes.
Slashes become hyphens, so \f[B]release/1\f[R] groups under
\f[B]release\-1\f[R].
Labels can collide; the atomically allocated evaluation leaf remains
distinct.
\f[B]time\f[R] uses UTC \f[B]YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ\f[R]; \f[B]token\f[R]
contains 128 bits of cryptographic randomness.
.PP
\f[B]Breaking change:\f[R] former flat attestation paths and normalized
per\-repository JSON summaries are no longer written or refreshed.
No fixed\-path alias replaces them.
Existing legacy files and historical logs remain untouched and are not
migrated.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/generated/<content\-key>/scan\-config.json\f[R]
Scan configuration written by the \f[B]Generate\f[R] RPC.
Records the matched requirement IDs so the \f[B]Scan\f[R] RPC can
synthesize passing assessments for requirements with no findings.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/generated/<content\-key>/\f[R]
Merged AMPEL policy bundle generated from granular policy files.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/generated/generate\-index.json\f[R]
Maps each generated target to its content\-keyed scan configuration and
policy bundle.
.TP
\f[I].complytime/ampel/granular\-policies/\f[R]
Default directory for granular AMPEL policy source files.
Overridden by \f[B]ampel_policy_dir\f[R] or complypack content.
.SH EVIDENCE
The provider records \f[B]Evidence\f[R] entries on each
\f[B]AssessmentLog\f[R] returned by the \f[B]Scan\f[R] RPC.
Two attestation types are produced per repository/branch/spec
combination:
.TP
\f[B]Snappy attestation\f[R] (type: \f[B]IntotoAttestation\f[R])
The \f[B]snappy\f[R] tool collects raw API data from the repository
platform (e.g., GitHub branch protection settings) and produces an
in\-toto attestation.
This attestation captures the observed state of the repository at scan
time.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Coordinate\f[R]: workspace\-root\-relative path to the
retained snappy attestation
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Digest\f[R]: \f[B]sha256:<hex>\f[R] digest of the
attestation file
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.ReferenceID\f[R]: \f[B]snappy_<token>\f[R], matching its
mapping reference
.RE
.TP
\f[B]Ampel attestation\f[R] (type: \f[B]IntotoAttestation\f[R])
The \f[B]ampel\f[R] tool evaluates granular security policies against
the snappy\-collected data and produces an in\-toto attestation
containing the evaluation results.
A non\-zero exit code indicates policy failures, not tool errors.
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Coordinate\f[R]: workspace\-root\-relative path to the
retained ampel attestation
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.Digest\f[R]: \f[B]sha256:<hex>\f[R] digest of the
attestation file
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Source.ReferenceID\f[R]: \f[B]ampel_<token>\f[R], matching its
mapping reference
.RE
.PP
\f[B]Breaking change:\f[R] evidence and reference IDs identify artifact
instances, not future evaluations of the same target.
Evidence IDs use \f[B]snappy_<token>\f[R] and \f[B]ampel_<token>\f[R].
Both artifacts use the evaluation\(cqs UTC collection\-start time for
\f[B]CollectedAt\f[R].
Many assessments share these files rather than creating copies.
Three scans retain three independent attestation pairs per
repository/branch/spec, including valid failing\-compliance results and
byte\-identical repeated outputs.
.PP
The Gemara Evaluation Log alone defines the evidence set.
Read its evidence mappings, resolve each coordinate against the selected
workspace root, and compare the stored file\(cqs SHA256 digest with the
log.
Mapping references retain escaped absolute \f[B]file://\f[R] URLs
identifying the same files, including percent\-encoding for spaces,
percent signs and non\-ASCII names.
A collection spec describes data collection; it is not a policy
identity.
Metadata and other unreferenced files are supporting outputs, not
implicit evidence.
.PP
\f[B]Workspace selection:\f[R] unchanged \f[B]complyctl\f[R] chooses
\f[B]\(enworkspace\f[R], then \f[B]COMPLYTIME_WORKSPACE\f[R], then its
invoking directory.
The provider receives that root in Generate global/Scan target
\f[B]workspace\f[R] variables.
Legacy/direct callers omitting the variable use the provider\(cqs
current directory.
All targets in a request must agree on the root.
Relative provider input/configuration paths now resolve against that
workspace; absolute paths stay absolute.
This is a breaking change for invocation\-relative configurations.
No process\-wide directory change is required.
.PP
Local preservation is always on, without retention settings, cleanup,
compression or deduplication.
Storage grows with every evaluation\(cqs output plus filesystem and
metadata overhead; interrupted attempts can leave unreferenced partial
files.
Metadata write/close failures warn without withholding complete,
verified evidence.
Required output, parse, digest or ownership/mode failures withhold the
affected evidence.
.PP
Fresh evaluation directories use \f[B]0700\f[R] and evidence/supporting
files use \f[B]0600\f[R], owned by the provider process\(cqs effective
UID/GID.
Existing parents must permit traversal and the workspace must be
writable by that execution account.
Normal scans are user\-owned; direct\-root and sudo scans stay
root\-owned.
The original non\-root user needs authorized elevated access to read and
hash root\-owned files; there is no automatic ownership handoff or
access grant.
For permission or disk\-space errors, check execution\-account access,
parent traversal and available capacity.
Do not widen evidence permissions or alter earlier owners to bypass
these checks.
.PP
Preserving or uploading the Evaluation Log alone does not preserve or
transfer its referenced bytes.
Downstream integrations own storage, transfers, access policy and
longevity: keep the log and its selected files retrievable and verify
transferred bytes against the recorded digests.
Collection and optional packaging remain future complyctl capabilities.
Any moved artifacts need mapping to their original references without
rewriting the log.
Absolute URLs remain origin\-specific after relocation;
workspace\-relative coordinates can resolve against a copied,
layout\-preserving root.
.PP
Upgrade stops refreshing fixed\-path outputs, leaves legacy files/logs
unchanged without migration and cannot recover evidence overwritten
before upgrade.
Rollback to an older provider resumes its overwrite behavior for new
scans; retained evaluation directories remain readable by authorized
readers.
.SH EXIT CODES
This provider is a gRPC subprocess managed by \f[B]complyctl\f[R].
Exit codes follow the hashicorp/go\-plugin protocol and are not
user\-facing.
